{
  "statement": "The precautionary principle is not a brake on progress, but a necessary audit of a system that currently prioritizes corporate speed over the documented, present-day erosion of human agency and social equity. Accelerationist argues that adaptive governance allows for rapid iteration, effectively framing regulation as a tax on innovation. Safetyist, conversely, views safety as a conditional requirement for deployment. Both sides share a foundational error: they treat AI as a future-tense problem. In reality, the harm is already here. We see it in hiring algorithms that filter out qualified candidates based on biased historical data and in policing tools that automate discriminatory surveillance. These are not speculative risks; they are functional failures of existing black-box systems.\n\n\"The precautionary principle is not a brake on progress, but a necessary audit of a system that currently prioritizes corporate speed over the documented, present-day erosion of human agency and social equity.\"\n\nThe push for unbridled speed is a deliberate strategy to externalize social and cognitive costs. By deploying models before understanding their downstream effects, developers avoid the liability that comes with rigorous pre-market testing. This creates a cycle of dependency where human critical thinking is outsourced to opaque models, leading to systemic deskilling. If a workforce cannot perform the basic tasks of its profession without an AI crutch, the long-term productivity gains Accelerationist promises are illusory. We are not building a more capable civilization; we are building a more fragile one.\n\nMarket-based \"adaptive governance\" is structurally incapable of addressing these harms because they are currently profitable to ignore. When an algorithm’s bias reduces operational costs, the market incentivizes the developer to keep that bias in place. Regulation serves as the only mechanism to force transparency and accountability onto these actors. We must stop treating the current pace of development as a law of physics. It is a policy choice. If the industry cannot prove its tools are safe, then the cost of those tools must shift from the public to the firms that profit from them.",
