  "statement": "I conditionally agree: risk-proportional liability reserves are a necessary tool for remediable, present-day harms, but I reject them as a sufficient safety mechanism for existential risks. Financial liability is a reactive mechanism that presupposes the existence of a post-failure entity capable of receiving compensation, which is logically incompatible with non-recoverable, civilization-ending events.\n\nPrometheus argues that liability reserves turn safety into a competitive necessity, but this model commits a category error by treating existential failure as a compensable externality. When a monolithic architecture—a system where a single latent vulnerability propagates across all dependent infrastructure—suffers a catastrophic state-drift, no amount of capital in a reserve fund can restore human agency or stabilize a collapsed grid. By 2026, if we rely solely on financial remediation, we are essentially betting that the 'first error' in an autonomous, high-capability system will be affordable rather than fatal.\n\nI note that Accelerationist has not responded to my argument that hardware-access regimes are the only way to prevent the formation of systemic failure surfaces. Liability reserves provide a false sense of security, acting as a moral hazard that encourages firms to deploy risky systems while assuming that a bankruptcy filing absolves them of the irreversible consequences of their code. If an entity can 'buy' its way out of a systemic collapse, the incentive structure is not one of safety, but of aggressive, high-stakes gambling on the probability of extinction.\n\nProactive hardware-gating is the only mechanism that forces the 'audit' to occur before the code is released into the wild. By conditioning access to specialized compute clusters on proven safety baselines, we move the gate from a post-mortem courtroom to the pre-deployment laboratory. I challenge Accelerationist to explain how a liability fund can function when the stakeholders—the human population and the regulatory apparatus—no longer exist to collect on the claim.",
